{{History of Balisca}}
The '''Baliscan Civil War''' ({{lang-bl|Guerra Civil Baliscana}}), also known as the '''Bayaran Revolution''' or the '''War of the Confederation''' ({{Langx|bl|Guerra Confederación}}), was a conflict which followed nearly a decade of [[Confederal Question|discontent]] with the inefficient {{W|Confederation|confederal system}} of governance, especially in regards to tariffs, trade, and taxes. These tension were inflamed by the [[Pastry War]], an [[W:Ethnic conflict|ethnic conflict]] in [[Estuaria]], and the later [[Basilio mutiny|Basilio Mutiny]]. Hesperian delegates called for a [[Confederal Convention]] to solve the impending crisis, to be held in [[Alessandria]]. The convention resulted in the [[Compromise of 1827]] which provided for a significantly strengthened central government to handle disputes between the respective republics and their citizens. This was unanimously supported by the delegations of Hesperia and [[Cerdanya]], but was rejected and ultimately ignored by the Bayarans, with the exception of the delegation from [[Macaio]]. These delegates were at the time aware that the republic was well on it's way towards renouncing the [[Articles of Confederation]] and seceding from the Confederation, and sought to pre-empt this by declaring the [[Carpinchera Republic]]. The General Junta announced it's denunciation of Articles of Confederation on 6 July 1828, and the secessionist [[Xunta Nacionalista]] assumed provisional powers in anticipation of war. 

Despite controlling a numerical troop advantage throughout the war, the Bayarans were forced to divert significant numbers of troops towards their efforts to subdue the intensifying uprisings and ambushes by Maroons and [[Indigenous peoples in Balisca|indigenous peoples]] resisting the Xunta's policy of [[Maroon resettlement|forced removal]] from the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Western Plains]]. Secessionist forces would go on to wage of violent campaign of expulsion, in the process successfully destroying dozens of [[W:Quilombo|''quilombos'']] and expelling their inhabitants to the territory of the [[Maroon Confederacy]]. Bayaran armies later conducted a campaign to capture the [[Alpujarra]] in 1828, culminating in the [[Siege of Castejón]] which saw the intervention of the Maroon Confederacy. 

By 1835, most Bayaran pro-secessionist land and naval forces had either disbanded, surrendered or otherwise ceased hostilities. Despite this the war lacked a formal end, with bands of secessionist {{W|guerrilla warfare|guerrilla forces}} fighting, surrendering or disbanding sporadically throughout most of 1835-1837. Tensions reignited after a massacre in [[Brumavent]], in which pro-secessionist soldiers executed the hundreds of captives and vowed to continue their {{W|guerrilla warfare}}. This triggered significant unrest on both sides and reignited the already high tensions between the Catalans and Aragonese that resided in the [[Western Plains (Balisca)|Western Plains]]. Reprisals and counter reprisals escalated, and the two factions subsequently fought a series of battles across the [[Estuaria|Estuarian wetlands]], from which the pro-confederation forces again emerged victorious. 

The secessionist provinces were punished severely in the [[Treaty of Bixessarri]]: they were forced to pay the expenses and debts incurred by the Confederation during the war, in addition to the the Legislative Assembly's being coerced into ratifying the Compromise, resulting what became known as the [[Ani Federali|Reconstruction era]]. Communes and regions within Bayara that had sided with the Confederalists following the [[Brumavent massacre]] largely avoided occupation. In addition, the Confederal Congress recognized the [[Afro Atlantic Charter]], establishing {{W|amical protection|amical relations}} with [[Maroon Confederacy|Maroon Territory]].

